ENCS 5412 switch — ESXi HOST bundle          EXPERIMENTAL
=========================================================

This directory ships inside the bootstrap VM but is NOT meant to run here.
Copy it to the ESXi host and run it there.

    scp -r /opt/encs-esxi root@<esxi>:/vmfs/volumes/datastore1/
    ssh root@<esxi> 'sh /vmfs/volumes/datastore1/encs-esxi/install.sh'

These three scripts HAVE been run on a real ENCS 5412 under ESXi 8.0 U3,
including a full uninstall/reinstall round trip. The bootstrap VM they set the
stage for is a different story: an IOMMU fault kills it on every run and the
switch only comes up when the firmware upload wins that race (once in three
attempts here). The full walkthrough, with every step graded by how far it can
be trusted, is docs/ESXI.md in the repository. Read both the IOMMU section and
the revert section before you start.

Run the scripts with `sh <path>` rather than executing them directly — files
on a VMFS datastore do not always carry the execute bit.

They are also exercised against a fake esxcli by scripts/66-test-esxi.py in
the repository: dry run writes nothing, install/uninstall is a bit-identical
round trip, and no write command ever names vSwitch0. The fake's output shapes,
option names and PATH were corrected against 8.0 U3 after the first real run —
which failed on its first line and found four things the old fake could not.

WHY IT IS SMALLER THAN THE PROXMOX BUNDLE
-----------------------------------------
ESXi cannot run the switch tools. encs-switch-tui is Python + curses,
encs-switch-api is bash + curl, and the shell here is busybox ash with no
bash, no systemd and a stripped Python. So the tools stay in the bootstrap
VM — where they already are, at /opt/encs-host — and the VM gets a second
vNIC on a portgroup tagged VLAN 2363 to reach the ASIC at 169.254.1.0.

That leaves the host with exactly two jobs, and they are what is here:

    passthrough    give the Marvell 11ab:be00 device to the bootstrap VM
    networking     one vSwitch on the te2 X710 function, a portgroup per VLAN

    front gi0-gi7 -> Marvell ASIC -> te1/te2 10G -> host X710 -> vSwitchENCS
                        ^ PCIe                                       |
                        |                                    encs-mgmt-2363
                  bootstrap VM  <-------------------------------------+
                  (boots the ASIC, and on this platform manages it too)

CONTENTS
--------
install.sh          passthrough + vSwitch + portgroups. Prints a plan and
                    stops; --yes applies it. Records everything it creates in
                    /etc/encs-esxi/created.
uninstall.sh        removes exactly what is in that record, in an order that
                    keeps you logged in. Refuses to remove a portgroup that
                    still has VMs on it.
encs-esxi-vnet      the host half of the NFVIS lan-net model: one portgroup
                    per VLAN. Prints the matching encs-switch-vnet command to
                    run in the VM, because neither half works alone.

WHAT IT NEVER TOUCHES
---------------------
vSwitch0, vmk0, any portgroup it did not create, and any VM. The ESXi
management VMkernel port belongs on the I210 MGMT CPU jack — the one link
that depends on no ASIC, no VM and no VLAN, and the way back in when the
switch side goes wrong. If yours is already on an X710 port, move it before
running any of this.

THE TWO THINGS MOST LIKELY TO BITE
----------------------------------
1. Passthrough reset. ESXi refuses devices it cannot reset cleanly. On the
   5412 it did not need help — 8.0 U3 picked "Bridge reset" itself and took
   the device live with no reboot. If yours will not power on,
   install.sh --reset-method bridge adds an /etc/vmware/passthru.map entry
   (try flr, d3d0, link, bridge in that order).

2. /etc is restored from the bootbank at boot, and auto-backup.sh only saves
   files that already have a ".#<name>" marker beside them. passthru.map has
   one because it ships with ESXi; a file you create does not, and is dropped
   on the next reboot no matter how often you run auto-backup.sh. That is why
   the record lives here on the datastore and not under /etc. If you edit
   passthru.map by hand, run /sbin/auto-backup.sh or the change reverts.

AND ONE THAT IS NOT SPECIFIC TO ESXi
------------------------------------
The loader can only bootstrap an ASIC in WFI. Re-running it against a switch
that is already up wedges it, and the only recovery is physical AC removal —
a host reboot is not enough, the ASIC is on standby power. So: no HA restart
policy on the bootstrap VM, and check `ping 169.254.1.0` before power-cycling
it. A wedged loader does not mean a dead switch; the ASIC keeps forwarding.
